  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nSynoptically, for Wednesday through next Sunday, our upper \nlevel flow remains mainly from the northwest for the majority of\nthe period. Starting around Wednesday, our flow will begin to \ntransition to mainly zonal as a weak upper level ridge passes \nthrough. By Saturday, as the ridge makes its way to the Eastern \nCONUS, our flow will transition to come from the southwest \nthrough Sunday. Friday through Sunday, Guidance indicates \npotential shortwaves moving through the County Warning Area \n(CWA). Sunday is where Guidance begins to diverge on the upper \nlevel pattern. The majority of the long range models show an \nupper level trough beginning to move into the region, but \nguidance presents multiple solutions regarding the strength and \ntrajectory. With this being roughly seven days out, it will be \nsomething to monitor as we move through the week. \n\nOverall, high temperatures across the Tri-State area should remain \nin the 70s and 80s. Tuesday is currently forecast to be the warmest \nday of the period, with highs in the low to mid 80s. Guidance, \nincluding the Canadian-NH and GFS, show a boundary or front moving \nthrough the region from the northwest Tuesday into Wednesday. As \nthis boundary moves through, temperatures should \"cool\" down to the \nupper 70s and low 80s before Saturdays highs briefly warm back to \nthe low to mid 80s. Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) are forecast \nto remain under 5% throughout the period. However, depending on the \nstrength of any shortwaves that pass through, PoPs could potentially \nincrease.",
